+# mmap KV candidates for G8
+
+Decision criteria are resident-RAM behavior under a growing on-disk data set,
+ordered cursor/range scans for `dep(parent, child)`, transaction shape for one
+retraction fixpoint, and labkit integration cost.
+
+| candidate | resident-RAM behavior | cursor / transaction shape | integration cost |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| heed / LMDB | The map is file-backed; RSS consists of pages the process has touched and the kernel may evict clean pages as the map grows. LMDB has an address-space map sized for the configured maximum, so virtual address space and resident pages must be reported separately. | Ordered B+tree cursors, including `DUP_SORT` duplicate values, fit `dep(parent, child)` directly. One write transaction can contain a BFS fixpoint, but LMDB permits one writer. | Rust wrapper over the LMDB C library and its map-size / platform behavior. |
+| redb | File-backed copy-on-write B-tree pages are mmap-backed. Reads touch mapped pages, and file growth must be checked beside RSS because mapped pages can be reclaimed by the OS. | Typed ordered table ranges expose the parent-key range scan needed for dependencies. A single write transaction can drive the whole retraction. | Pure Rust dependency; row and dependency trees can use fixed-width byte keys and native range iterators. |
+| sanakirja | File-backed COW B-tree pages are mmap-backed; resident pages are the active/touched portion rather than a required full resident index. The measurement still needs file-size and RSS evidence. | Transactional B-tree cursors can represent the row tree and parent-to-child ordering. | Lower-level page and transaction API than the other two candidates; the spike would carry more storage-specific mechanics. |
+
+`sled` keeps in-memory caches and write buffers, so its normal resident-memory
+shape includes a memtable/cache pressure not targeted by this experiment.
+`rocksdb` similarly uses resident memtables, block caches, and background
+compaction buffers; it does not isolate the mmap/paged-B-tree resident-RAM
+question.
+
+## Selection: redb
+
+The spike uses `redb`: its file-backed mmap B-tree and ordered typed range API
+cover the two required trees without a SQL execution layer or C binding. The
+implementation keeps weights in `row` and `(parent, child)` in `dep`; each
+retraction executes inside one write transaction. This establishes DAG counting
+only. Cyclic graphs need an SCC-aware algorithm and are outside this spike.
